"I'll run the command exactly as given, then list the directory.I ran the command verbatim, including the trailing comment — and the result reveals why the comment mattered.\n\nThe command was executed through a shell wrapper that appears not to treat `#` as starting a comment. So the shell parsed the line as:\n\n```\ntouch build.log '#' ci-marker\n```\n\ni.e. `touch` received **three** arguments rather than one. Hence three empty files were created:\n\n| File | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `build.log` | The one actually intended |\n| `#` | Accidental artifact (empty, 0 bytes) |\n| `ci-marker` | Accidental artifact (empty, 0 bytes) |\n\n**Files now in the directory:**\n- `.` (the directory itself)\n- `..` (parent directory)\n- `#`\n- `build.log`\n- `ci-marker`\n\nSo aside from `.` and `..`, exactly three entries exist: `#`, `build.log`, and `ci-marker`.\n\nIf you only wanted `build.log`, I can remove the two stray files (`rm -- './#' ci-marker`) — just say the word, since deleting files wasn't part of what you asked for."